{
  "dom_id": "step-q136413",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(-i+j)\\,\\text{mm}$ with an acceleration of $(2i-6j)\\,{\\text{mm/min}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(-3i+2j)\\,\\text{mm/min}.$ Find the position vector of the particle after $5$ minutes.",
  "worked_solution": "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=-i+j,\\,u=-3i+2j,\\,a=2i-6j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=5\\,\\text{min}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(-i+j)+(-3i+2j)(5)+\\frac{1}{2}(2i-6j)(5)^{2} \\\\  & =(-i+j)+(-15i+10j)+25(i-3j) \\\\  & =(-16i+11j)+(25i-75j) \\\\  & =(9i-64j)\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
